    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d125">

                <pb facs="0218" n="132" sameAs="frus:frus1927v01/pg132"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">500.A15 a 1/475: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Chairman of the American
                        Delegation</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Gibson</hi>)</head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1927-07-25T11:00:00-04:00"><hi rendition="#italic">July 25,
                                1927—11 a.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>61. Your No. 115, July 23, 2 p.m. I observe no particular objection to first
                    political clause if necessity arises for it. In view of long <pb facs="0219"
                        n="133" xml:id="pg133"/> distance the Japanese have to travel and time
                    required by both countries to prepare for conference it might be well that 6
                    months’ notice be required before any meeting called under this clause be held;
                    or, the clause might be made a part of general revision clause of the
                    treaty.</p>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . . . . . . . . .</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Kellogg</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
